            "title": "Security, privacy and incident response",
            "lessons": [
              {
                "id": "01-01",
                "title": "Security, privacy and incident response",
                "kind": "reading",
                "xp": 10,
                "blocks": [
                  {
                    "type": "html",
                    "html": "<h2>Learning objectives</h2>"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "html",
                    "html": "<p>By the end of this module you will be able to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Explain confidentiality, integrity and availability, and map an incident to the property it threatens.</li>\n<li>Triage logs into a timeline, separating evidence from speculation.</li>\n<li>Contain an exposed secret, rotate it, and verify the exposure is closed.</li>\n<li>Write an incident record and recommend hardening, logging and privacy controls with cost and risk.</li>\n</ol>"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "html",
                    "html": "<h2>The week&#x27;s idea</h2>"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "html",
                    "html": "<p>The estate has been built, named, secured with identity and permissions, and given a\nbackup. This week it is attacked, or at least exposed. A configuration page served the\ndatabase password to anyone who asked. The security log shows someone asked, twice, after\nrepeated failed logins. The incident is already in progress when the module starts.</p>\n<p>Security is not a product you install. It is a set of decisions made with incomplete\ninformation: what is exposed, who could reach it, what the evidence proves, what you change\nfirst, and what you accept as residual risk. The lab gives you a small, real incident. You\nread the log, reproduce the exposure, contain it, rotate the secret, verify the fix and\nwrite the record. The record is the artefact an auditor, a customer or a regulator will\nread.</p>"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "html",
                    "html": "<h2>Key ideas</h2>"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "html",
                    "html": "<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Idea</th>\n<th>What to hold in your head</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Confidentiality</td>\n<td>Only authorised subjects can read the data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Integrity</td>\n<td>The data is accurate and has not been changed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Availability</td>\n<td>The service is there when it is needed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Threat</td>\n<td>A potential cause of harm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vulnerability</td>\n<td>A weakness a threat can use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk</td>\n<td>The combination of likelihood and impact</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Attack surface</td>\n<td>Everything an attacker can reach</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Defence in depth</td>\n<td>More than one control between the attacker and the asset</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Least privilege</td>\n<td>The smallest access that does the job</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Secret</td>\n<td>A credential that grants access and must not be public</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rotation</td>\n<td>Replacing a secret and retiring the old value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Log</td>\n<td>A time-ordered record of events</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Log integrity</td>\n<td>Evidence that the log has not been altered</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Personal data</td>\n<td>Information about an identified or identifiable person</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Incident response</td>\n<td>Prepare, detect, contain, eradicate, recover, learn</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evidence</td>\n<td>The material that supports the account of what happened</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Notification</td>\n<td>Telling the people and bodies who need to know</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Residual risk</td>\n<td>The risk that remains after the controls</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "html",
                    "html": "<h2>The CloudCore ticket</h2>"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "html",
                    "html": "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>From:</strong> Sophia Martines, Chief Information Security Officer\n<strong>Priority:</strong> critical\n<strong>Service:</strong> customer portal and the estate secret store</p>\n<p>A customer sent us a screenshot of <code>http://portal.estate.local/admin/config.txt</code>. The\npage shows the database password and an admin token. The security log shows repeated\nfailed logins for <code>admin</code> and two successful requests to that path from the same address,\n<code>203.0.113.45</code>.</p>\n<p>I need the exposure closed, the credential rotated with evidence that the old value no\nlonger works, the scope of the incident, and an incident record with the decisions, the\nnotifications and the prevention work.</p>\n<p>Do not delete the log. It is evidence.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The ticket is a sequence of decisions, not a single fix. Closing the page is containment.\nRotating the secret is eradication. Proving the old value fails is recovery. The record is\nhow the organisation learns.</p>"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "01-02",
                "title": "The 3AM page",
                "kind": "story",
                "xp": 30,
                "blocks": [
                  {
                    "type": "story",
                    "src": "stories/the-3am-page.json",
                    "start": "",
                    "intro": "<p>The portal is timing out for some customers. It is 03:12. Every choice\nchanges what you can prove later.</p>",
                    "debrief": "<p>Which decision protected the evidence? Which one made the next failure\neasier to explain? Write the one sentence you would add to the incident\nrecord.</p>"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "01-03",
                "title": "Checkpoint: evidence, containment, record",
                "kind": "checkpoint",
                "xp": 25,
                "blocks": [
                  {
                    "type": "checkpoint",
                    "questions": [
                      {
                        "id": "q1",
                        "prompt": "What is the first move when an incident is still developing?",
                        "options": [
                          "Establish impact and preserve evidence",
                          "Restart the service to clear the alert",
                          "Close the incident until a customer complains",
                          "Change the firewall rules"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0,
                        "explanation": "You need to know who is affected and keep the evidence before you change the system."
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "q2",
                        "prompt": "Why is \"restart it first\" dangerous?",
                        "options": [
                          "It can destroy the logs and state that explain the failure",
                          "It always makes the outage longer",
                          "It changes the customer's network",
                          "It is slower than capturing evidence"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0,
                        "explanation": "A restart can remove the very evidence you need for the cause and the incident record."
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "q3",
                        "prompt": "What is containment?",
                        "options": [
                          "Stopping the spread or the bleeding while you investigate",
                          "Deleting the affected account",
                          "Writing the final report",
                          "Rebuilding the whole estate"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0,
                        "explanation": "Containment limits damage. It is not the same as finding and removing the cause."
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "q4",
                        "prompt": "What belongs in an incident record?",
                        "options": [
                          "Impact, timeline, evidence, cause and the change that prevents a repeat",
                          "Only the final status code",
                          "The names of everyone on the team",
                          "The vendor's marketing page"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0,
                        "explanation": "The record is for the next responder, not for the person who was there."
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "q5",
                        "prompt": "The service is stable but you do not know the cause. What should you do?",
                        "options": [
                          "Record the gap, keep the evidence and keep investigating",
                          "Declare the incident closed and forget it",
                          "Restart the service again",
                          "Remove the logs to save space"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0,
                        "explanation": "A stable service is not the same as a solved incident. Record what you do not know."
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
